  "53d91338-a5fc-43fe-9d36-131695711b02": {
    "id": "53d91338-a5fc-43fe-9d36-131695711b02",
    "topic": "Explain the concept of gravity",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-09T09:22:13.224689",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-09T09:22:37.147481",
    "video_url": "/videos/MathExplanationScene.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.000\nWelcome to this lesson on understanding gravity.\n\n00:05.000 --> 00:10.000\nGravity is a force that attracts objects with mass towards each other.\n\n00:10.000 --> 00:14.000\nLet's look at some examples.\n\n00:14.000 --> 00:18.000\nFirst, we have an apple falling from a tree.\n\n00:18.000 --> 00:21.000\nThe Earth's gravity pulls the apple down.\n\n00:21.000 --> 00:25.000\nNext, consider the Earth orbiting the Sun.\n\n00:25.000 --> 00:28.000\nThe Sun's gravity keeps the Earth in orbit.\n\n00:28.000 --> 00:32.000\nAnother example is the Moon orbiting the Earth.\n\n00:32.000 --> 00:36.000\nThe Earth's gravity keeps the Moon in its orbit around us.\n\n00:36.000 --> 00:41.000\nLet's summarize the key points.\n\n00:41.000 --> 00:44.000\nGravity is a force of attraction.\n\n00:44.000 --> 00:48.000\nIt acts between objects with mass.\n\n00:48.000 --> 00:51.000\nAnd it holds planets in orbit.\n\n00:51.000 --> 00:54.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "0d4c8ef1-406f-44ad-967f-d9195b4a08d8": {
    "id": "0d4c8ef1-406f-44ad-967f-d9195b4a08d8",
    "topic": "rotational motion",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-09T11:17:10.551456",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-09T11:18:14.023091",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251209_111814.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251209_111814.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.000\nWelcome to this video on rotational motion.\n\n00:04.000 --> 00:07.000\nLet's explore what rotational motion is all about.\n\n00:07.000 --> 00:12.000\nRotational motion is motion around a fixed axis. Think of things that are spinning!\n\n00:12.000 --> 00:15.000\nNow let's look at some examples.\n\n00:15.000 --> 00:19.000\nOur first example is a wheel. Notice how it spins around its center.\n\n00:19.000 --> 00:23.000\nHere's another example: the Earth. The Earth rotates around its axis.\n\n00:23.000 --> 00:27.000\nFor our third example, we have a CD. See how it spins around the center hole?\n\n00:27.000 --> 00:36.000\nLet's summarize the key points. First, rotational motion is motion around an axis. Second, examples include wheels and planets.\n\n00:36.000 --> 00:40.000\nAnd third, it uses angles to describe its movement.\n\n00:40.000 --> 00:42.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "6f39c727-2088-4b99-82e0-1b10fbc59ce1": {
    "id": "6f39c727-2088-4b99-82e0-1b10fbc59ce1",
    "topic": "Explain how to relax when I in a problem",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-09T11:21:03.721814",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-09T11:22:08.658050",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251209_112208.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251209_112208.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.000\nWelcome! Let's learn how to\nrelax when you're facing a problem.\n\n00:04.000 --> 00:09.000\nWhat does it mean to relax\nin a problem? It's about\nmanaging stress and finding\ncalm during challenges.\n\n00:09.000 --> 00:15.000\nLet's look at some examples.\nFirst, try deep breathing.\nInhale slowly and\nexhale completely.\n\n00:15.000 --> 00:20.000\nAnother example: take a break.\nStep away from the problem\nand clear your head.\n\n00:20.000 --> 00:26.000\nFinally, practice positive self-talk.\nChange \"I can't do this\"\nto \"I can try my best.\"\n\n00:26.000 --> 00:32.000\nTo summarize the key points:\nBreathe deeply, take breaks,\nand stay positive.\n\n00:32.000 --> 00:35.000\nNow you're equipped to relax\nwhen facing problems!\n\n00:35.000 --> 00:37.000\nThis video was created by\nApilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "9ce6d6a2-dbc5-444a-bc5d-3e56deba389e": {
    "id": "9ce6d6a2-dbc5-444a-bc5d-3e56deba389e",
    "topic": "Explanation of how rotational motion works, covering angular velocity, torque, and moment of inertia.",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-09T21:53:02.867866",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-09T21:55:07.556662",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251209_215507.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251209_215507.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.000\nWelcome to this lesson on rotational motion.\n\n00:04.000 --> 00:06.000\nLet's explore angular velocity,\n\n00:06.000 --> 00:08.500\ntorque, and moment of inertia.\n\n00:08.500 --> 00:12.000\nAngular velocity is the rate of change\nof angular displacement.\n\n00:12.000 --> 00:15.500\nTorque is a rotational force causing\nangular acceleration.\n\n00:15.500 --> 00:20.000\nMoment of inertia is the resistance\nto change in rotational motion.\n\n00:20.000 --> 00:26.000\nThe relationship between these is Torque\nequals moment of inertia times angular acceleration.\n\n00:26.000 --> 00:30.000\nLet's summarize the key points.\n\n00:30.000 --> 00:34.000\nFirst, angular velocity describes how\nfast something rotates.\n\n00:34.000 --> 00:37.000\nSecond, torque is a twisting force.\n\n00:37.000 --> 00:40.000\nAnd third, moment of inertia is the\nresistance to twisting.\n\n00:40.000 --> 00:41.700\nNow you understand rotational motion!\n\n00:41.700 --> 00:44.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "37a6cefc-6829-4ba0-9229-839b03de2989": {
    "id": "37a6cefc-6829-4ba0-9229-839b03de2989",
    "topic": "Explain the concept of gravity",
    "level": "special_topic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-12T22:00:06.916426",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-12T22:01:23.506137",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251212_220123.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251212_220123.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.000\nWelcome to this lesson on understanding gravity.\nLet's explore what gravity means and how it works.\n\n00:05.000 --> 00:13.000\nGravity is defined as a force that attracts objects with mass toward each other.\n\n00:13.000 --> 00:17.000\nNow let's look at our first example: an apple falling.\n\n00:17.000 --> 00:20.000\nGravity pulls the apple downwards.\n\n00:20.000 --> 00:25.000\nHere's another example: planets orbiting the sun.\n\n00:25.000 --> 00:29.000\nGravity keeps the planets in their orbits around the sun.\n\n00:29.000 --> 00:34.000\nFor our third example, consider ocean tides.\nThe moon's gravity causes tides on Earth.\n\n00:34.000 --> 00:43.000\nLet's summarize the key points. First, gravity attracts objects with mass.\nSecond, gravity determines planetary orbits. And third, gravity causes tides on Earth.\n\n00:43.000 --> 00:45.000\nNow you understand gravity.\n\n00:45.000 --> 00:47.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "4a5bd400-e7eb-4ab6-8bdd-05a1db480ede": {
    "id": "4a5bd400-e7eb-4ab6-8bdd-05a1db480ede",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:09:56.960240",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:11:54.769716",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_161154.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_161154.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.400\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where it goes after it falls?\n\n00:05.400 --> 00:12.900\nIt's all part of something called the water cycle! Basically, it's water moving around the Earth in a continuous loop.\n\n00:12.900 --> 00:23.000\nFirst, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans. This turns the water into a gas called water vapor and floats up into the air. This is called evaporation.\n\n00:23.000 --> 00:31.800\nAs the water vapor rises, it cools down and clumps together to form clouds. This is condensation.\n\n00:31.800 --> 00:42.100\nWhen the clouds get too full of water droplets, they release the water back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. This is precipitation.\n\n00:42.100 --> 00:51.400\nThe water then flows back into rivers, lakes, and eventually the ocean, and the whole cycle starts again!\n\n00:51.400 --> 00:59.200\nSo, rain from the sky goes into a river, evaporates, forms a cloud, and then rains again. Pretty cool, right?\n\n00:59.200 --> 01:06.500\nThe water cycle is how water is constantly recycled on our planet. This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "8ed4e3e9-1b2c-46be-be4b-1896607aaf53": {
    "id": "8ed4e3e9-1b2c-46be-be4b-1896607aaf53",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:10.170088",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:13:11.277535",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_161311.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_161311.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.700\nHey everyone, welcome! Today we're talking about tests.\n\n00:04.700 --> 00:12.400\nWhat are they and why do we use them?\n\n00:12.400 --> 00:19.300\nSimply put, a test is a way to find out what you know or what you can do.\n\n00:19.300 --> 00:25.500\nThink of it like this: your teacher might give you a math test to see how well you understand adding and subtracting.\n\n00:25.500 --> 00:31.700\nOr, a chef might taste their soup to test if it needs more salt.\n\n00:31.700 --> 00:39.600\nThe test helps them learn something. So, tests aren\u2019t always about getting a grade. Sometimes, they\u2019re about checking if something works, like testing a new toy to make sure it's safe.\n\n00:39.600 --> 00:42.300\nOther times, they help us figure out what we need to learn next.\n\n00:42.300 --> 00:45.200\nBasically, a test is just a tool for learning and improving.\n\n00:45.200 --> 00:45.900\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "254dfe16-44f3-4280-ad1c-1b83e33bbf0c": {
    "id": "254dfe16-44f3-4280-ad1c-1b83e33bbf0c",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:12.172908",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:15:21.913073",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_161521.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_161521.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.800\nHey everyone, welcome! Today we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:04.800 --> 00:13.900\nNow, a test is simply a way to check something. Think of it like asking a question to see if you know the answer, or trying something out to see if it works.\n\n00:13.900 --> 00:19.100\nThe main idea is to find out information.\n\n00:19.100 --> 00:26.700\nWe use tests all the time! Your doctor might give you a test to see if you're healthy.\n\n00:26.700 --> 00:33.200\nOr, a baker might taste the cake batter to test if it's sweet enough.\n\n00:33.200 --> 00:39.000\nImagine you're building with blocks. You might test to see if a tower is strong enough by gently pushing on it.\n\n00:39.000 --> 00:47.100\nIf it falls, you know you need to build it differently! Tests help us learn and improve. So, to recap, a test is a way of checking something, finding out information, and seeing if things work like we expect.\n\n00:47.100 --> 00:50.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "1b35a542-d993-4cd3-a2be-0f047cf99ad7": {
    "id": "1b35a542-d993-4cd3-a2be-0f047cf99ad7",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:15.154754",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:17:08.053096",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_161707.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_161707.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.200\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:04.200 --> 00:08.400\nNow, what is a test?\n\n00:08.400 --> 00:18.200\nSimply put, a test is a way to find out something. It helps us understand if something is true, or how well something works.\n\n00:18.200 --> 00:24.200\nThink of it like this: you want to know if a cake is done. You might use a toothpick to test it.\n\n00:24.200 --> 00:29.300\nIf the toothpick comes out clean, the cake is ready! That's a test!\n\n00:29.300 --> 00:33.500\nOr, maybe you want to see if you remember your ABCs.\n\n00:33.500 --> 00:37.700\nSomeone might ask you to recite them. That's a test too!\n\n00:37.700 --> 00:44.900\nTests can be written, spoken, or even involve doing something. They help us learn and improve.\n\n00:44.900 --> 00:53.000\nWhether it's a cake, your ABCs, or something much bigger, tests are all about finding things out.\n\n00:53.000 --> 00:56.000\nSo, remember, a test is a way to discover information or check something. This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "b80debc2-6aa1-47dc-b60f-a39c3eae9d8e": {
    "id": "b80debc2-6aa1-47dc-b60f-a39c3eae9d8e",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:17.055834",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:18:45.566578",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_161845.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_161845.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.899\nAlright, let's talk about tests! What exactly is a test?\n\n00:04.899 --> 00:12.200\nSimply put, a test is a way to find out something.\n\n00:12.200 --> 00:16.099\nThink of it like an experiment, but a little more structured.\n\n00:16.099 --> 00:19.999\nWe use tests all the time.\n\n00:19.999 --> 00:24.699\nWant to know if a cake is done? You poke it with a toothpick - that's a test!\n\n00:24.699 --> 00:33.300\nTrying to figure out if a lightbulb works? You screw it in and flip the switch \u2013 another test!\n\n00:33.300 --> 00:38.400\nIn science and school, tests are a little more formal.\n\n00:38.400 --> 00:45.600\nThey help us understand if an idea is true, or if you\u2019ve learned something new.\n\n00:45.600 --> 00:52.300\nFor example, a math test checks if you understand addition, and a science experiment tests if your hypothesis is correct.\n\n00:52.300 --> 00:56.700\nSo remember, a test is just a way to learn something.\nWhether it's baking a cake or proving a theory, tests help us explore the world around us.\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
