  "fe13c037-6876-4332-86d4-ee59a594094a": {
    "id": "fe13c037-6876-4332-86d4-ee59a594094a",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:25.032197",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:28:11.473344",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_162811.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_162811.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.800\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or how puddles disappear?\n\n00:05.800 --> 00:14.600\nIt's all thanks to something called the water cycle. Basically, the water cycle is how water moves all around the Earth.\n\n00:14.600 --> 00:26.600\nIt's a continuous loop with a few key steps. First, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into vapor. We call this evaporation.\n\n00:26.600 --> 00:34.600\nThis water vapor then rises into the air and cools down, forming clouds. This is condensation.\n\n00:34.600 --> 00:45.300\nWhen the clouds get too full of water, it falls back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. That's precipitation!\n\n00:45.300 --> 00:57.500\nThink about it: when it rains, the water might flow into a stream, which leads to a river, eventually reaching the ocean. Or, some of the rain soaks into the ground.\n\n00:57.500 --> 01:04.700\nThen, the whole process starts all over again. So, evaporation, condensation, and precipitation\n\n01:04.700 --> 01:17.100\n\u2013 that's the water cycle in a nutshell! This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "11878a2f-878d-4824-86af-a068aa323628": {
    "id": "11878a2f-878d-4824-86af-a068aa323628",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:25.700550",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:29:22.348957",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_162922.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_162922.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.600\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, and where it goes after?\n\n00:05.600 --> 00:14.500\nIt's all thanks to something called the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves all around our planet.\n\n00:14.500 --> 00:25.300\nIt starts with evaporation \u2013 that's when the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans and turns it into vapor, like steam.\n\n00:25.300 --> 00:35.800\nThis vapor rises up into the air. As it rises, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. This is called condensation.\n\n00:35.800 --> 00:48.900\nWhen enough water droplets gather together in the clouds, they get too heavy and fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. This is precipitation!\n\n00:48.900 --> 01:00.400\nThen, the water flows back into rivers, lakes, and oceans, and the whole cycle starts all over again.\n\n01:00.400 --> 01:13.800\nSo, the water you drink today might have been part of a dinosaur's bath millions of years ago! It\u2019s a continuous process of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.\n\n01:13.800 --> 01:18.500\nPretty cool, right? This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "d214bb80-5f7c-42a2-b84c-d91868beb59b": {
    "id": "d214bb80-5f7c-42a2-b84c-d91868beb59b",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:26.196518",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:30:28.486921",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163028.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163028.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.200 --> 00:05.500\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or how lakes stay full?\n\n00:05.500 --> 00:13.400\nIt's all thanks to the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves around our planet.\n\n00:13.400 --> 00:21.600\nIt's like a big, continuous loop. First, the sun heats up water in oceans, lakes, and rivers.\n\n00:21.600 --> 00:38.400\nThis turns the water into vapor, which rises into the air \u2013 that's called evaporation. As the water vapor goes higher, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets or ice crystals, forming clouds.\n\n00:38.400 --> 00:50.800\nThis is called condensation. When enough of these droplets come together, they become heavy enough to fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n\n00:50.800 --> 01:03.400\nThat's precipitation! Then, the water flows into rivers and streams, eventually making its way back to the oceans and lakes, ready to evaporate again.\n\n01:03.400 --> 01:12.100\nThink of a puddle disappearing on a sunny day, or rain watering your garden. They are both part of the water cycle.\n\n01:12.100 --> 01:19.500\nSo, the water cycle is all about evaporation, condensation, and precipitation, constantly moving water from the Earth to the sky and back again.\n\n01:19.500 --> 01:23.100\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "643500a2-79c7-4563-a351-3a0ab4e6fd92": {
    "id": "643500a2-79c7-4563-a351-3a0ab4e6fd92",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:26.627245",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:32:01.914056",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163201.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163201.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:06.600\nHey there, future scientists! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where it goes?\n\n00:06.600 --> 00:13.400\nIt's all thanks to the water cycle! Basically, it's water's amazing journey around our planet.\n\n00:13.400 --> 00:24.100\nFirst, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into vapor \u2013 that's called evaporation.\n\n00:24.100 --> 00:34.500\nThink of it like steam from a hot shower! This vapor rises up into the air and cools down, forming clouds - that's condensation.\n\n00:34.500 --> 00:45.500\nWhen the clouds get too full of water, it falls back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail \u2013 we call that precipitation!\n\n00:45.500 --> 00:54.700\nThis water then flows into bodies of water, seeps into the ground, or is used by plants.\n\n00:54.700 --> 01:05.800\nAnd guess what? The whole process starts all over again! So, rain falls, water evaporates, clouds form, and it repeats endlessly.\n\n01:05.800 --> 01:14.500\nIt's like a big, beautiful circle! The water cycle keeps our planet healthy and provides us with the water we need to live.\n\n01:14.500 --> 01:19.800\nPretty cool, right? This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
