  "97a69a91-ca65-49a3-8201-c9c438547eaa": {
    "id": "97a69a91-ca65-49a3-8201-c9c438547eaa",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:27.559177",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:34:12.884612",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163412.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163412.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:06.300\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where it goes after it falls?\n\n00:06.300 --> 00:15.000\nIt's all part of something called the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves around our planet.\n\n00:15.000 --> 00:26.700\nIt starts with evaporation. That's when the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into vapor \u2013 like steam!\n\n00:26.700 --> 00:36.500\nThen, that water vapor rises up into the air and cools down. This is called condensation, and it forms clouds.\n\n00:36.500 --> 00:51.200\nThink of it like when you see steam on a cold window. When enough water condenses in the clouds, it falls back to Earth as precipitation, like rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n\n00:51.200 --> 01:07.900\nAnd the cycle starts all over again! So, rain fills up rivers, snow melts and flows into streams, and eventually, all that water makes its way back to the oceans, ready to be evaporated again.\n\n01:07.900 --> 01:17.300\nThe water cycle is what keeps our planet alive and provides us with the water we need. This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "cb421613-ae77-4dc4-aa42-dc7d4b6eaf43": {
    "id": "cb421613-ae77-4dc4-aa42-dc7d4b6eaf43",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:28.001919",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:35:13.079237",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163512.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163512.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.200\nHey everyone, and welcome! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where puddles disappear to?\n\n00:07.200 --> 00:13.000\nIt's all thanks to the water cycle! Think of it as a big, never-ending loop.\n\n00:13.000 --> 00:22.700\nFirst, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into a gas called water vapor. This process is called evaporation.\n\n00:22.700 --> 00:28.700\nThe water vapor rises up into the air. As it goes higher, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. This is called condensation.\n\n00:28.700 --> 00:39.000\nWhen these water droplets get too heavy, they fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail \u2013 that's precipitation!\n\n00:39.000 --> 00:49.900\nThis water then flows into rivers and lakes, or soaks into the ground, eventually making its way back to the ocean.\n\n00:49.900 --> 00:59.000\nAnd then, the whole cycle starts again! So, from the ocean to the clouds and back again, that's the amazing water cycle in action.\n\n00:59.000 --> 01:02.600\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "ab8f1e7f-fddf-40f1-9cd2-bcc949683159": {
    "id": "ab8f1e7f-fddf-40f1-9cd2-bcc949683159",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:29.132346",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:36:50.683227",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163650.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163650.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:09.100\nHey there! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where it goes after it falls?\n\n00:09.100 --> 00:18.300\nIt\u2019s all part of a super important process called the water cycle.\n\n00:18.300 --> 00:31.100\nBasically, it's how water moves all around our planet. First, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans.\n\n00:31.100 --> 00:42.000\nThis turns the water into a gas called water vapor, which floats up into the air. This is called evaporation.\n\n00:42.000 --> 00:54.300\nAs the water vapor rises, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. This is condensation.\n\n00:54.300 --> 01:05.800\nWhen enough water droplets gather in the clouds, they become too heavy and fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. That's precipitation!\n\n01:05.800 --> 01:16.500\nThe water then flows back into rivers, lakes, and eventually the ocean, ready to start the cycle all over again.\n\n01:16.500 --> 01:27.300\nThink of it like this: the sun's energy turns water into a gas, the gas forms clouds, and the clouds release the water back to the Earth. It\u2019s a never-ending loop!\n\n01:27.300 --> 01:30.800\nSo, evaporation, condensation, and precipitation \u2013\n\n01:30.800 --> 01:34.600\nthat\u2019s the water cycle in a nutshell.\n\n01:34.600 --> 01:38.200\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "d5e63792-c174-4886-93fe-78ea2801fa93": {
    "id": "d5e63792-c174-4886-93fe-78ea2801fa93",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:30.195676",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:37:46.546590",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_163746.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_163746.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:08.600\nHey everyone, and welcome! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or what happens to water after you drink it? It's all part of an amazing process called the water cycle.\n\n00:08.600 --> 00:15.800\nThink of it as water's endless journey.\n\n00:15.800 --> 00:25.500\nFirst, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into a gas called water vapor. This is called evaporation.\n\n00:25.500 --> 00:38.300\nThen, the water vapor rises up into the sky, cools down, and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. We call this condensation.\n\n00:38.300 --> 00:50.900\nWhen enough water droplets come together in the clouds, they get heavy and fall back to earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. This is precipitation.\n\n00:50.900 --> 01:02.700\nThe water then flows into rivers, lakes, and eventually back to the ocean, ready to start the cycle all over again.\n\n01:02.700 --> 01:13.600\nSo, the water cycle is a continuous loop of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation. Pretty cool, right?\n\n01:13.600 --> 01:17.200\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "fdeff5df-cd49-44e5-90eb-989b546fccec": {
