    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164017.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.000\nHey there, have you ever wondered where rain comes from, or where it goes?\n\n00:02.000 --> 00:12.800\nIt's all part of something called the water cycle! Imagine water moving around our planet in a big circle.\n\n00:12.800 --> 00:21.300\nIt starts with the sun warming up water in lakes, rivers, and even the ocean.\n\n00:21.300 --> 00:28.100\nThis warming turns the water into a gas called water vapor, which rises into the air \u2013 that's evaporation.\n\n00:28.100 --> 00:36.600\nAs it goes higher, the water vapor cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. This is called condensation.\n\n00:36.600 --> 00:49.700\nWhen these droplets get too heavy, they fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail \u2013 that's precipitation!\n\n00:49.700 --> 00:59.700\nThink about it: rain falling on a mountain, flowing into a river, the river leading to the ocean, and then the ocean water evaporating again.\n\n00:59.700 --> 01:08.300\nThe water cycle is how water is constantly being recycled. It's a never-ending journey that keeps our planet alive!\n\n01:08.300 --> 01:11.300\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "bd3db8f0-8347-42df-a06a-2360b4a0419f": {
    "id": "bd3db8f0-8347-42df-a06a-2360b4a0419f",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:32.516326",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:41:32.436805",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_164132.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164132.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.800\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or how puddles disappear?\n\n00:05.800 --> 00:14.600\nIt's all thanks to the amazing water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves all around our planet.\n\n00:14.600 --> 00:27.000\nFirst, there's evaporation. That's when the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into vapor, like steam. This vapor rises up into the air.\n\n00:27.000 --> 00:38.000\nAs it rises, the water vapor cools and turns back into tiny liquid droplets, forming clouds! This is called condensation.\n\n00:38.000 --> 00:49.500\nThen, when the clouds get full of water, it falls back to earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. This is precipitation!\n\n00:49.500 --> 01:00.300\nThe water then flows back into rivers, lakes, and oceans, and the cycle starts all over again.\n\n01:00.300 --> 01:12.000\nSo, the water cycle is a continuous loop of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation, constantly moving water around the Earth.\n\n01:12.000 --> 01:17.600\nIt's pretty cool, right? This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "85ca0124-be59-4932-8fb1-9dbe5e15ca44": {
    "id": "85ca0124-be59-4932-8fb1-9dbe5e15ca44",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:33.214979",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:42:30.231533",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_164230.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164230.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.800\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or how puddles disappear? \n\n00:05.800 --> 00:14.400\nIt's all thanks to something called the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves around our planet.\n\n00:14.400 --> 00:24.100\nFirst, the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into a gas called water vapor. This is called evaporation.\n\n00:24.100 --> 00:29.600\nThat water vapor rises up into the air. As it rises, it cools down and turns back into tiny liquid water droplets, forming clouds.\n\n00:29.600 --> 00:40.100\nThis is called condensation. When those droplets get too heavy, they fall back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail. That's called precipitation!\n\n00:40.100 --> 00:50.900\nThink about it like this: The sun warms the ocean, creating clouds. Those clouds then rain on your garden, and eventually that water flows back into a river, heading back to the ocean!\n\n00:50.900 --> 01:05.400\nThe water cycle is a continuous loop, constantly recycling water all around us. So next time you see a puddle, remember the amazing water cycle!\n\n01:05.400 --> 01:08.900\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "609cbe1c-94fb-4a38-8894-327689d956f7": {
    "id": "609cbe1c-94fb-4a38-8894-327689d956f7",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:34.018602",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:44:17.249177",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_164417.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164417.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:06.300\nHey everyone! Ever wonder where rain comes from, or where it goes after it falls?\n\n00:06.300 --> 00:15.200\nIt's all thanks to something called the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves all around our planet.\n\n00:15.200 --> 00:27.800\nIt starts with evaporation. That's when the sun heats up water in lakes, rivers, and oceans, turning it into vapor, which rises up into the air.\n\n00:27.800 --> 00:38.900\nAs the water vapor rises, it cools down and turns back into tiny water droplets, forming clouds. This is called condensation.\n\n00:38.900 --> 00:49.700\nWhen enough of these droplets gather together in the clouds, they become heavy and fall back down to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n\n00:49.700 --> 00:59.000\nThis is called precipitation. Then the water flows back into lakes, rivers, and oceans, ready to start the whole cycle all over again!\n\n00:59.000 --> 01:09.800\nSo, the sun heats it, it evaporates, condenses into clouds, and then precipitates back down. That's the water cycle in a nutshell!\n\n01:09.800 --> 01:13.400\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "ab96f2db-2d98-445f-b957-e7a3a3f18f97": {
    "id": "ab96f2db-2d98-445f-b957-e7a3a3f18f97",
    "topic": "Explain the water cycle",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:10:34.993836",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:45:32.950374",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_164532.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164532.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:09.500\nHey everyone, ever wondered how water gets from the sky to the ground and back again?\n\n00:09.500 --> 00:20.900\nIt's all thanks to something called the water cycle! Basically, the water cycle is how water moves around our planet.\n\n00:20.900 --> 00:29.800\nIt starts with the sun heating up water in oceans, lakes, and rivers. This heat turns the water into a gas called water vapor, which rises into the air.\n\n00:29.800 --> 00:38.400\nWe call this evaporation. As the water vapor goes higher, it cools down and turns back into tiny liquid water droplets or ice crystals.\n\n00:38.400 --> 00:53.100\nThese droplets join together to form clouds, which is called condensation. When the clouds get too full of water, it falls back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n\n00:53.100 --> 01:05.700\nThat's precipitation! Then, the water flows into rivers and lakes, eventually making its way back to the ocean, and the whole cycle starts all over again.\n\n01:05.700 --> 01:14.300\nThink about it like this: rain filling a puddle, the sun drying it up, and then the clouds bringing more rain.\n\n01:14.300 --> 01:26.300\nThat's the water cycle in action! So, evaporation, condensation, and precipitation \u2013 that\u2019s how water keeps moving around our planet.\n\n01:26.300 --> 01:29.900\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "fcd9263b-b34a-439b-8464-cb6aa13c32db": {
    "id": "fcd9263b-b34a-439b-8464-cb6aa13c32db",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:12:50.408053",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T16:46:15.373847",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_164615.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_164615.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.000\nHi everyone, and welcome!\nToday we're diving into something super important: tests.\n\n00:07.000 --> 00:14.500\nThink of a test as a way to check if something is working correctly, or to see how well you understand a topic.\nIt's like a little experiment!\n\n00:14.500 --> 00:22.900\nFor example, you might \"test\" a new lightbulb to see if it turns on.\nOr, your teacher might give you a quiz to \"test\" your knowledge of math.\n\n00:22.900 --> 00:34.000\nTests can be simple, like pressing a button to see if a machine starts.\nOr they can be more complex, like a science experiment with lots of steps.\n\n00:34.000 --> 00:47.300\nSo, remember, a test is just a way to find out information or check if something works.\nWhether it's a lightbulb or your understanding of a subject, testing helps us learn and improve.\n\n00:47.300 --> 00:58.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
