  "2999006a-9e59-4e5b-8d45-81673d2e5d39": {
    "id": "2999006a-9e59-4e5b-8d45-81673d2e5d39",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T16:14:15.468227",
    "updated_at": "2025-12-31T19:32:39.310177",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20251231_193239.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20251231_193239.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.900\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're going to talk about tests \u2013 and not the kind you take in school.\n\n00:07.900 --> 00:21.500\nIn computer programming, a test is a way to check if your code is working correctly. Think of it like this: you build something, and then you need to make sure it does what it's supposed to do.\n\n00:21.500 --> 00:29.400\nTests are small pieces of code that automatically run your program and compare the results to what you expect.\n\n00:29.400 --> 00:38.200\nFor example, if you write a function to add two numbers, a test would check if 2 + 2 actually equals 4.\n\n00:38.200 --> 00:41.800\nIf it does, great! The test passes.\n\n00:41.800 --> 00:53.800\nIf it doesn't, something's wrong, and you know you need to fix your code. Basically, tests help you catch mistakes early and make sure your programs are reliable.\n\n00:53.800 --> 01:04.000\nThey're a super important part of writing good code. So, remember, tests are your friends, helping you build better programs.\n\n01:04.000 --> 01:07.600\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
