  "2731a3ca-b1e9-4631-85ca-d1a07f898d07": {
    "id": "2731a3ca-b1e9-4631-85ca-d1a07f898d07",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2025-12-31T20:36:34.147430",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-01T08:54:25.511283",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260101_085425.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260101_085425.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.700\nHi everyone, and welcome! Today, we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:05.700 --> 00:16.600\nNo need to worry, it's not that kind of test! In computer programming, a test is simply a way to check if your code is working correctly.\n\n00:16.600 --> 00:26.800\nThink of it like this: you build a toy car, and you need to make sure it drives. The test is driving the car and seeing if it goes forward.\n\n00:26.800 --> 00:41.400\nSo, a program is like the toy car, and a test is like driving it. For example, if you write code to add two numbers, a test would check if 2 plus 2 actually equals 4.\n\n00:41.400 --> 00:47.600\nIf the test passes, great! If not, you know there's a problem with your code.\n\n00:47.600 --> 01:00.600\nTesting helps you find and fix mistakes early on, making your programs more reliable. In short, tests are mini-experiments that prove your code does what it's supposed to do.\n\n01:00.600 --> 01:04.100\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
