    "updated_at": "2026-01-01T14:59:03.763156",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260101_145903.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260101_145903.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.700\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:02.700 --> 00:06.700\nToday we're going to talk about tests \u2013\n\n00:06.700 --> 00:12.300\nnot the kind you cram for, but the kind that helps us check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:12.300 --> 00:19.400\nBasically, a test is a way to see if something does what it's supposed to do.\n\n00:19.400 --> 00:25.600\nThink of it like this: you want to know if your flashlight works.\n\n00:25.600 --> 00:28.800\nThe test? You turn it on!\n\n00:28.800 --> 00:32.100\nIf it lights up, the test passes.\n\n00:32.100 --> 00:34.800\nIf it doesn't, the test fails.\n\n00:34.800 --> 00:40.200\nAnother example is baking a cake.\n\n00:40.200 --> 00:44.500\nThe test might be tasting it to see if it's sweet enough.\n\n00:44.500 --> 00:46.600\nOr checking if it rose properly.\n\n00:46.600 --> 00:51.600\nSo, in short, a test is just a method for checking if something works as expected.\n\n00:51.600 --> 00:52.500\nIt gives us confidence that things are running smoothly.\n```"
  },
