    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260102_033018.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.200 --> 00:05.400\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we\u2019re going to talk about tests.\n\n00:05.400 --> 00:18.600\nNow, you might think of school tests, but in the world of, say, science or computer programming, a test is really just a way of checking if something works like it should.\n\n00:18.600 --> 00:25.000\nIt\u2019s like asking a question and seeing if you get the right answer.\n\n00:25.000 --> 00:33.800\nSo, how does it work? Well, you create a set of conditions or inputs, and then you see if the output or result matches what you expected.\n\n00:33.800 --> 00:43.800\nFor example, if you're testing a lightbulb, you screw it in and flip the switch. If the light turns on, the test passes!\n\n00:43.800 --> 00:49.800\nIf it doesn\u2019t, then the test fails.\n\n00:49.800 --> 00:54.500\nAnother example could be a taste test!\n\n00:54.500 --> 00:59.200\nYou try a cookie and decide if it tastes good.\n\n00:59.200 --> 01:04.100\nThat\u2019s your test!\n\n01:04.100 --> 01:16.000\nBasically, tests help us find problems and make sure things are working correctly. They're super important for building things that are reliable and do what they're supposed to.\n\n01:16.000 --> 01:26.100\nSo, remember, a test is just a way of checking if something works the way we expect it to.\n\n01:26.100 --> 01:26.800\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
