  "febc178d-4c28-4caa-a1b1-51673018773b": {
    "id": "febc178d-4c28-4caa-a1b1-51673018773b",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T01:52:06.807760",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-02T07:47:13.162532",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260102_074713.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260102_074713.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.600\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're going to talk about something super important: Tests!\n\n00:07.600 --> 00:17.800\nSimply put, a test is a way to check if something works correctly.\n\n00:17.800 --> 00:26.700\nThink of it like this: You want to know if your new bike is safe to ride. So, you test it by riding it around a little bit.\n\n00:26.700 --> 00:34.200\nIn the world of computers, tests are just as important.\n\n00:34.200 --> 00:50.800\nWe write little pieces of code that automatically check if our programs are doing what they're supposed to do. For example, if we write a program that's supposed to add two numbers, a test would give it \"2 + 2\" and make sure the answer is \"4.\"\n\n00:50.800 --> 01:02.200\nIf it's not, the test fails, and we know there's a problem to fix! So, tests are like a safety net, making sure everything works as expected.\n\n01:02.200 --> 01:06.000\nThey help us catch mistakes early and build more reliable software.\n\n01:06.000 --> 01:09.600\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
