    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260102_105425.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.500\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:02.500 --> 00:06.100\nToday we're going to talk about tests \u2013\n\n00:06.100 --> 00:10.500\nnot the kind you take in school, but the kind that help us check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:10.500 --> 00:17.000\nThink of a test like a little detective. It asks a question, and then looks for the answer to see if everything is okay.\n\n00:17.000 --> 00:24.700\nFor example, if you\u2019re baking a cake, you might use a toothpick test. You stick the toothpick in, and if it comes out clean, the cake is done.\n\n00:24.700 --> 00:26.100\nThat's a test!\n\n00:26.100 --> 00:30.800\nAnother simple example is checking if a light bulb works.\n\n00:30.800 --> 00:34.800\nYou flip the switch; if the light comes on, the test is passed!\n\n00:34.800 --> 00:37.400\nIf it stays dark, the test failed.\n\n00:37.400 --> 00:42.800\nTests are all about checking to see if things are doing what they're supposed to do.\n\n00:42.800 --> 00:48.600\nSo, remember, a test is simply a way to check if something works.\n\n00:48.600 --> 00:53.300\nIt asks a question, and we look for the answer to see if everything is alright.\n\n00:53.300 --> 00:56.000\nThis video was created by\n\n00:56.000 --> 00:56.800\nApilage\n\n00:56.800 --> 00:57.000\nAI.\n```"
  },
