  "4f606e47-9483-4332-aa7d-e494686afd5c": {
    "id": "4f606e47-9483-4332-aa7d-e494686afd5c",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T13:50:18.846899",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-02T17:10:11.736216",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260102_171011.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260102_171011.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.200\nOkay, let's talk about tests! \nWhat exactly is a test?\n\n00:05.200 --> 00:14.800\nSimply put, a test is a way to find something out. \nIt's how we check if something is true, or how we measure something.\n\n00:14.800 --> 00:20.600\nThink of it like this: you want to know if a cake is done baking.\nThe test?\n\n00:20.600 --> 00:27.000\nYou poke it with a toothpick.\nIf the toothpick comes out clean, the cake passes the test!\nIt's done!\n\n00:27.000 --> 00:33.000\nAnother example: a doctor might give you a test to see if you're healthy.\nThey might check your temperature or listen to your heart.\n\n00:33.000 --> 00:39.900\nThese are all tests that give them information.\nSo, a test is just a method for gathering information or checking something.\n\n00:39.900 --> 00:49.200\nWe use tests all the time in everyday life!\n\n00:49.200 --> 00:52.700\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "ce11412b-6555-4ca6-b57e-dfefc1d71f63": {
    "id": "ce11412b-6555-4ca6-b57e-dfefc1d71f63",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T13:50:19.290566",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-02T17:10:59.947396",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260102_171059.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260102_171059.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.400\nHey there, welcome! Today we're talking about tests.\n\n00:04.400 --> 00:15.800\nNow, in everyday life, a test is simply a way to check something, right? Like testing a new recipe or testing the water before you jump in.\n\n00:15.800 --> 00:26.100\nWell, in computer programming, a test is pretty similar. It's a piece of code that checks if another piece of code is working correctly.\n\n00:26.100 --> 00:40.500\nWe write these tests to make sure our programs do what we expect them to do. Think of it like this: if you write a program to add two numbers, a test would check if the program actually adds them correctly.\n\n00:40.500 --> 00:47.800\nFor example, if you give it 2 and 3, does it really give you 5? That's what a test confirms.\n\n00:47.800 --> 00:56.400\nSo, tests are like little detectives, making sure our code is behaving itself and giving us the right answers.\n\n00:56.400 --> 01:07.100\nThey help us find mistakes early and keep our programs reliable. In short, a test is a way to verify that a piece of code works as intended.\n\n01:07.100 --> 01:10.700\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
