  "f4209e30-2534-445f-b46f-97776494fbb6": {
    "id": "f4209e30-2534-445f-b46f-97776494fbb6",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:01:07.843782",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-03T08:50:59.690602",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260103_085059.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_085059.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.100\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're talking about tests.\n\n00:05.100 --> 00:15.800\nNow, you might think of tests as something you take in school, but really, a test is just a way to find out if something is true or working correctly.\n\n00:15.800 --> 00:21.600\nThink of it like this: You want to know if your lightbulb works.\n\n00:21.600 --> 00:26.400\nThe test? You flip the switch!\n\n00:26.400 --> 00:30.600\nIf the light comes on, the test passed.\n\n00:30.600 --> 00:34.800\nIf it doesn't, the test failed.\n\n00:34.800 --> 00:38.800\nAnother example? Imagine baking a cake.\n\n00:38.800 --> 00:42.800\nThe test is tasting it!\n\n00:42.800 --> 00:49.800\nIf it tastes good, the test passed. If it's burnt, well, the test didn't go so well.\n\n00:49.800 --> 00:56.400\nSo, a test is simply a procedure we use to check if something meets our expectations.\n\n00:56.400 --> 01:03.000\nWe perform an action and then observe the result. That result tells us if the \"thing\" passed or failed the test.\n\n01:03.000 --> 01:05.400\nPretty simple, right?\n\n01:05.400 --> 01:09.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "b377aa1a-76b2-4759-bfb5-17687c5ab5da": {
    "id": "b377aa1a-76b2-4759-bfb5-17687c5ab5da",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:01:08.557877",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-03T08:51:41.059557",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260103_085140.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_085140.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.200 --> 00:02.400\nOkay, let's talk about tests!\n\n00:02.400 --> 00:05.000\nIn simple terms, a test is just a way to find out something.\n\n00:05.000 --> 00:7.000\nIt's how we check if something is working, or if something is true.\n\n00:07.000 --> 00:12.000\nThink of it like checking if a lightbulb works. You turn it on, and if it lights up, the test passes!\n\n00:12.000 --> 00:17.000\nAnother example is a spelling test. Your teacher gives you words, and you write them down.\n\n00:17.000 --> 00:21.000\nIf you spell them correctly, you pass that part of the test.\n\n00:21.000 --> 00:25.000\nTests aren't just in school, though. Doctors use tests to see if you're healthy,\n\n00:25.000 --> 00:29.000\nand engineers use tests to make sure bridges are strong.\n\n00:29.000 --> 00:38.000\nSo, remember, a test is simply a way of gathering information and checking something. It can be as simple as seeing if a lightbulb shines, or something more complex.\n\n00:38.000 --> 00:41.000\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "5667fb14-c1ae-43c3-9850-367b9f29fd4e": {
    "id": "5667fb14-c1ae-43c3-9850-367b9f29fd4e",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:01:09.321387",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-03T08:52:34.242844",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260103_085234.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_085234.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.200\nHey everyone, welcome! Today we're talking about tests, and no, not the kind you cram for.\n\n00:07.200 --> 00:22.200\nIn computer programming, a test is simply a way to check if your code is working correctly. Think of it like this: you build something with LEGOs, and then you try to play with it to make sure it doesn't fall apart.\n\n00:22.200 --> 00:29.200\nProgramming tests do the same thing. They run your code and see if it gives the expected result.\n\n00:29.200 --> 00:38.000\nFor example, if you write a program to add two numbers, a test would check if 2 + 2 actually equals 4. If it doesn\u2019t, then you know there's a problem!\n\n00:38.000 --> 00:46.700\nTests help you find mistakes early and make sure your code is reliable.\n\n00:46.700 --> 00:55.700\nSo, to recap, tests verify that your code does what it's supposed to do, helping you build better software.\n\n00:55.700 --> 00:59.300\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
