    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_121327.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.400\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:02.400 --> 00:04.200\nToday we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:04.200 --> 00:05.800\nWhat exactly is a test?\n\n00:05.800 --> 00:10.500\nSimply put, a test is a way to find out something, or to check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:10.500 --> 00:15.200\nThink of it like this: you take a spelling test at school to see how well you know your words.\n\n00:15.200 --> 00:19.000\nOr, a doctor might run tests to check if you're healthy.\n\n00:19.000 --> 00:20.800\nSee? Different kinds of tests, same idea.\n\n00:20.800 --> 00:26.000\nAnother example is when you bake a cake.\nYou might taste a little bit of the batter to test if it's sweet enough.\n\n00:26.000 --> 00:27.300\nThat's a test too!\n\n00:27.300 --> 00:31.600\nSo, a test is just a way of gathering information or checking something.\n\n00:31.600 --> 00:35.700\nIt helps us understand more about the world around us.\n\n00:35.700 --> 00:37.800\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "4ccf524d-14d0-4825-aabb-54031065066a": {
    "id": "4ccf524d-14d0-4825-aabb-54031065066a",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:03:34.760551",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-03T12:14:25.049333",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260103_121424.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_121424.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:07.300\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're going to explore something super important: tests.\n\n00:07.300 --> 00:18.700\nThink of a test as a way to check if something is working correctly, or if you understand something.\n\n00:18.700 --> 00:26.300\nIt's like checking if a lightbulb turns on when you flip the switch.\n\n00:26.300 --> 00:41.800\nIn programming, tests are small pieces of code that check if your other code is doing what it's supposed to do.\n\n00:41.800 --> 00:49.000\nFor example, let's say you write a program that's supposed to add two numbers together.\n\n00:49.000 --> 00:56.200\nA test would give it two numbers, like 2 and 3, and then check if the program gives you the correct answer, which is 5.\n\n00:56.200 --> 01:08.000\nIf it does, the test passes! If it doesn't, then something's wrong and you need to fix your code. Tests help you catch mistakes early and make sure your programs are reliable.\n\n01:08.000 --> 01:11.700\nSo, remember, a test is simply a way to check if something works as expected.\n\n01:11.700 --> 01:15.300\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
