  "d9129791-a310-43d5-8025-053c5f929ead": {
    "id": "d9129791-a310-43d5-8025-053c5f929ead",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:04:46.256504",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-03T14:10:09.484181",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260103_141009.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260103_141009.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.500\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:02.500 --> 00:11.000\nToday we're going to talk about tests \u2013 not the kind you stress about in school, but tests in software development.\n\n00:11.000 --> 00:19.800\nBasically, a test is a way to check if your code is working the way you expect it to. Think of it like a quality check.\n\n00:19.800 --> 00:26.200\nSo, how does it work? Well, you write code that specifically checks other code.\n\n00:26.200 --> 00:35.800\nFor example, if you write a function that's supposed to add two numbers, a test would make sure that 2 + 2 actually equals 4.\n\n00:35.800 --> 00:47.000\nThese tests can be really simple, like that addition example, or they can be more complex, checking how different parts of your program interact.\n\n00:47.000 --> 00:53.700\nThe important thing is that they give you confidence that your code is doing what it's supposed to do.\n\n00:53.700 --> 00:59.700\nThey also help you catch mistakes early!\n\n00:59.700 --> 01:05.000\nIn a nutshell, tests are like a safety net for your code, helping you build reliable and bug-free software.\n\n01:05.000 --> 01:08.500\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
