    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260104_135508.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260104_135508.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.000\nHi everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:05.000 --> 00:08.300\nToday we\u2019re going to talk about tests.\n\n00:08.300 --> 00:16.600\nNow, you might be thinking about school tests, but in this case, we're talking about a way to check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:16.600 --> 00:25.000\nThink of it like this: a test is a way to gather information to see if something passes or fails based on certain rules.\n\n00:25.000 --> 00:28.900\nFor example, if you\u2019re baking a cake, you might use a toothpick test.\n\n00:28.900 --> 00:32.800\nYou stick a toothpick in, and if it comes out clean, the cake is done!\n\n00:32.800 --> 00:33.900\nThat\u2019s a test.\n\n00:33.900 --> 00:39.400\nOr, imagine you built a toy car. You test it by letting it roll down a ramp to see if it goes straight.\n\n00:39.400 --> 00:40.500\nThat's another test!\n\n00:40.500 --> 00:51.700\nSo, a test is simply a process to see if something meets a certain standard or expectation. It helps us know if something is ready or needs more work.\n\n00:51.700 --> 00:53.700\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "2fda923b-f4c6-4801-8935-a23bc58fa5e4": {
    "id": "2fda923b-f4c6-4801-8935-a23bc58fa5e4",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:22:53.043458",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-04T13:55:56.210705",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260104_135556.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260104_135556.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.400\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:05.400 --> 00:13.500\nToday we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:13.500 --> 00:20.100\nNow, a test, in its simplest form, is just a way to check if something works the way it's supposed to.\n\n00:20.100 --> 00:30.100\nThink of it like this: you bake a cake and then you taste it. That taste is a test!\n\n00:30.100 --> 00:41.200\nYou're checking if it's sweet enough, if it's cooked through, and if it tastes good. In the world of technology, tests are similar.\n\n00:41.200 --> 00:49.700\nWe write small bits of code to check if larger pieces of code are working correctly.\n\n00:49.700 --> 00:58.200\nFor example, let's say we have a function that's supposed to add two numbers. A test would give it the numbers two and three, and then check if the answer is five.\n\n00:58.200 --> 01:06.700\nIf it is, the test passes. If not, something\u2019s wrong!\n\n01:06.700 --> 01:14.000\nTests help us find problems early, before they cause bigger issues.\n\n01:14.000 --> 01:17.500\nThey give us confidence that our code is reliable.\n\n01:17.500 --> 01:27.300\nSo remember, a test is simply a way to verify that something is working as expected.\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
