    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260104_233334.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260104_233334.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:01.200\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:01.200 --> 00:03.900\nToday we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:03.900 --> 00:10.800\nIn its simplest form, a test is really just a way to check if something works the way it's supposed to.\n\n00:10.800 --> 00:17.200\nThink of it like this: you have a new lightbulb, and you want to know if it lights up.\n\n00:17.200 --> 00:21.900\nThe test is screwing it into a lamp and flipping the switch.\n\n00:21.900 --> 00:25.700\nIf the light comes on, the test passes!\n\n00:25.700 --> 00:28.100\nIf it doesn't, the test fails.\n\n00:28.100 --> 00:34.500\nIt's the same idea with anything, from checking if your cookies are baked enough to making sure a new app on your phone works correctly.\n\n00:34.500 --> 00:38.400\nWe create tests to make sure things are doing what we expect them to do.\n\n00:38.400 --> 00:42.900\nSo, testing is all about checking and verifying.\n\n00:42.900 --> 00:47.300\nIt helps us catch mistakes early and make sure things are working as they should.\n\n00:47.300 --> 00:49.800\nThat's the basics of testing!\n\n00:49.800 --> 00:57.900\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
