  "18282c26-6b90-413a-bf65-88ebc7b697e6": {
    "id": "18282c26-6b90-413a-bf65-88ebc7b697e6",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:34:50.542714",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-05T05:48:36.417748",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260105_054836.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260105_054836.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:09.400\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we\u2019re going to talk about tests \u2013 and no, not the kind you dread taking in school.\n\n00:09.400 --> 00:21.700\nIn computer programming, a test is simply a way to check if your code is working correctly. Think of it like this: you build a toy car and you want to make sure it drives.\n\n00:21.700 --> 00:25.800\nYou test it by pushing it. If it rolls, great!\n\n00:25.800 --> 00:32.200\nIf it doesn't, you know something needs fixing. In code, tests do the same thing.\n\n00:32.200 --> 00:40.000\nWe write little pieces of code that automatically check if other parts of our code are behaving as expected.\n\n00:40.000 --> 00:47.800\nFor example, if you write a function that's supposed to add two numbers, a test would check if it actually adds them correctly.\n\n00:47.800 --> 00:56.600\nIt might try 2 + 2 and make sure the answer is 4. Tests help us find bugs early and make sure our programs are reliable.\n\n00:56.600 --> 01:03.500\nSo, remember, tests are your friend! They help you build better and more trustworthy software.\n\n01:03.500 --> 01:07.100\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
