  "db98b5b7-09b8-4d66-b58f-e6aa524341b4": {
    "id": "db98b5b7-09b8-4d66-b58f-e6aa524341b4",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:39:20.355711",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-05T11:28:56.592999",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260105_112856.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260105_112856.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.400\nHey everyone, and welcome! Today we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:05.400 --> 00:15.000\nNow, you might think of school exams, but in this case, a test is simply a way to check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:15.000 --> 00:23.100\nThink of it like this: you\u2019re making cookies, and you taste one to see if it needs more sugar. That\u2019s a test!\n\n00:23.100 --> 00:32.200\nIn software, a test does the same thing. It runs a piece of code and confirms that it produces the expected result.\n\n00:32.200 --> 00:40.900\nFor example, if you have code that adds two numbers, a test would check if 2 plus 2 actually equals 4.\n\n00:40.900 --> 00:49.300\nIf it does, the test passes! If it doesn't, something's wrong, and you know you need to fix your code.\n\n00:49.300 --> 01:01.000\nSo, tests are like little detectives, making sure everything is working as it should. They help catch mistakes early and ensure your programs are reliable.\n\n01:01.000 --> 01:04.600\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
  "fd664cfa-9b6d-45de-b060-d80b4f9a755a": {
