  "203140b6-619e-407e-b59e-271331ecd2c6": {
    "id": "203140b6-619e-407e-b59e-271331ecd2c6",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T14:42:05.391838",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-05T15:20:28.776114",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260105_152028.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260105_152028.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.400\nHey everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:04.400 --> 00:11.900\nToday we're going to talk about tests \u2013 not the kind you take in school, but tests in the world of computer programming.\n\n00:11.900 --> 00:23.500\nSimply put, a test is a way to check if your code is working the way you expect it to. Think of it like a detective, making sure everything is in its right place.\n\n00:23.500 --> 00:33.800\nFor example, let's say you write a piece of code that adds two numbers together. A test would check if the code actually adds those numbers correctly.\n\n00:33.800 --> 00:38.600\nSo, if you give it 2 and 3, the test would make sure it gives you back 5.\n\n00:38.600 --> 00:47.300\nTests are super important because they help you find mistakes early on, before they cause bigger problems.\n\n00:47.300 --> 00:52.400\nThey also give you confidence that your code is reliable.\n\n00:52.400 --> 00:59.900\nSo, remember, tests are your code's bodyguard, always making sure it's doing its job.\n\n00:59.900 --> 01:02.500\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
