    "updated_at": "2026-01-05T16:54:06.330663",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260105_165406.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260105_165406.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:02.700\nHello everyone, and welcome!\n\n00:02.700 --> 00:07.100\nToday we're going to explore something super important: the test.\n\n00:07.100 --> 00:11.500\nNow, a test, in its simplest form, is just a way to check something.\n\n00:11.500 --> 00:17.200\nThink of it like this: you want to know if your cake is done.\n\n00:17.200 --> 00:19.700\nYou might stick a toothpick in it.\n\n00:19.700 --> 00:21.300\nThat's a test!\n\n00:21.300 --> 00:24.500\nThe toothpick test tells you if the inside of the cake is cooked.\n\n00:24.500 --> 00:28.700\nSimilarly, in science, we use tests to see if a hypothesis is correct.\n\n00:28.700 --> 00:36.500\nFor example, if you think a plant grows faster with more sunlight, you can test it by giving some plants lots of sun and others less.\n\n00:36.500 --> 00:38.700\nThen you measure their growth.\n\n00:38.700 --> 00:46.900\nSo, a test is really just a way of gathering information and seeing if something is true or works the way you think it should.\n\n00:46.900 --> 00:54.400\nRemember to always think about what you're trying to check and how you can best do it.\n\n00:54.400 --> 00:56.600\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
