  "06b6800d-5d42-4d22-8958-33e1d618960a": {
    "id": "06b6800d-5d42-4d22-8958-33e1d618960a",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T15:07:14.825670",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-06T23:29:48.224341",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260106_232948.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260106_232948.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:08.700\nHey everyone, and welcome!\nToday we're going to talk about tests \u2013 and no, not the kind you cram for!\n\n00:08.700 --> 00:18.200\nIn computer programming, a test is basically a check.\nWe use them to make sure our code is working the way we expect it to.\n\n00:18.200 --> 00:31.900\nThink of it like this: You build a toy car, and you want to make sure it drives forward.\nThe test is simply, \"Does the car move forward when I turn it on?\"\nIf it does, great! The test passes.\nIf it doesn't, something is wrong, and we need to fix it.\n\n00:31.900 --> 00:37.600\nIn programming, we write code that performs these tests automatically.\n\n00:37.600 --> 00:48.700\nFor example, we might have a function that's supposed to add two numbers together.\nOur test would check if that function actually returns the correct sum.\n\n00:48.700 --> 00:55.900\nIf it does, the test passes.\nIf not, we know there's a bug in our code.\n\n00:55.900 --> 01:06.000\nTesting is super important because it helps us catch errors early and build reliable software.\n\n01:06.000 --> 01:16.400\nSo, remember, tests are simply checks that ensure our code is working correctly.\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
