  "8273a835-573b-432d-b50c-0ecf60a75dfe": {
    "id": "8273a835-573b-432d-b50c-0ecf60a75dfe",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T15:08:55.787769",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-07T01:19:59.462435",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260107_011959.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260107_011959.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:05.400\nHey everyone, and welcome!\nToday we\u2019re going to talk about tests.\n\n00:05.400 --> 00:16.200\nNo need to be nervous, these aren't the kind you take in school!\nIn software, a test is simply a way to check if something is working correctly.\n\n00:16.200 --> 00:23.200\nThink of it like taste-testing a cake.\nYou bake it, then you take a bite to see if it tastes good.\nThat's a test!\n\n00:23.200 --> 00:34.800\nIn programming, a test might check if a button on a website does what it's supposed to, or if a calculator program gives you the right answer.\nWe write little bits of code to automatically perform these checks.\n\n00:34.800 --> 00:48.000\nSo, if you have a program that adds two numbers, a test would check if 2 + 2 actually equals 4.\nSee?\n\n00:48.000 --> 00:50.200\nEasy peasy.\n\n00:50.200 --> 01:01.400\nEssentially, tests help us make sure our programs are reliable and do what we expect them to do.\nThey give us confidence that our code is working as it should.\n\n01:01.400 --> 01:10.500\nSo, remember, a test is just a way to check if something works.\nThis video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
