  "76138ab6-b2f6-4763-af98-b5c0861af4f5": {
    "id": "76138ab6-b2f6-4763-af98-b5c0861af4f5",
    "topic": "Test",
    "level": "basic",
    "status": "completed",
    "queue_position": 1,
    "created_at": "2026-01-01T15:16:25.886611",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-07T09:54:54.009411",
    "video_url": "/uploads/MathExplanationScene_20260107_095453.mp4",
    "error": null,
    "progress": "Completed!",
    "video_filename": "MathExplanationScene_20260107_095453.mp4",
    "subtitles": "```webvtt\nWEBVTT\n\n00:00.000 --> 00:04.900\nHello, and welcome! Today we're going to talk about tests.\n\n00:04.900 --> 00:14.800\nNow, you might think of school exams, but in programming, a test is simply a way to check if your code is working correctly.\n\n00:14.800 --> 00:26.200\nThink of it like a quality control check. We write small pieces of code, called tests, that run our main code and see if the results are what we expect.\n\n00:26.200 --> 00:35.600\nFor example, if you write a function that's supposed to add two numbers, a test would check if 2 + 2 actually equals 4.\n\n00:35.600 --> 00:45.600\nIf the answer is correct, the test passes. If not, the test fails, telling you there's a problem somewhere in your code.\n\n00:45.600 --> 00:57.900\nTests help catch mistakes early and make sure your programs do what they're supposed to do. So, tests are small programs used to make sure our big programs work correctly.\n\n00:57.900 --> 01:04.600\nThat's it for today's quick intro to tests! This video was created by Apilage AI.\n```"
  },
